# Westside Cowboy, Gurriers, Lime Garden lead stacked Dot To Dot 2027 line-up

> 🎵 *Curated Music Story from NME Music Official Publications*

### 📖 The Full Story

Music News Westside Cowboy, Gurriers, Lime Garden lead stacked Dot To Dot 2027 line-up Other names announced include Turnspit, KNATS and The Joy Hotel By Liberty Dunworth | 26th November 2026 Westside Cowboy and Gurriers. CREDIT: Derek Bremner for NME

The first wave of names for Dot To Dot 2027 have been revealed, with Westside Cowboy , Gurriers and Lime Garden leading the way.

The new announcement comes following a sold-out edition in 2026, and the festival being shortlisted for Inner-City Festival Of The Year, Line-Up Of The Year at the UK Festival Awards and Best Independent Festival at Access All Areas.

### 🎤 Key Highlights & Community Reactions

It is set to return to Bristol on Saturday May 29 and Nottingham on Sunday May 30, and today (Thursday November 26), the first wave of names have been announced.

Westside Cowboy are at the top of the bill, just three years after forming and shortly after they released their highly anticipated debut album ‘ It Goes On ’ earlier this year. Their slot on the line-up follows them winning Glastonbury’s prestigious Emerging Talent Competition, being crowned BBC Introducing Artist Of The Year 2026 and becoming NME Cover stars .

Also joining them at the top of the line-up are fellow NME Cover stars Gurriers, who returned this year with their album ‘ Nobody’s Coming To Save You ’, and Brighton four-piece Lime Garden, who dropped the critically acclaimed sophomore album ‘ Maybe Not Tonight ’ earlier this year.

“We’re delighted to have Westside Cowboy as our much-deserved headliners for 2027 and welcome them back to Dot To Dot Festival after their incredible late-night set in 2025,” said Conrad Rogan, booker for Dot To Dot Festival.

“After another sold-out edition last year, we are immensely proud to book another line-up showcasing the best emerging talent from across the world to Bristol and Nottingham across the May bank holiday. Our ethos is always to champion and nurture these acts, and giving them a platform in front of audiences that match that energy is something we never take for granted.”

